> Hi Ni,
>
> I do not have a particular source handy, but I have done this before in some work that has code available online. For example, lines 80-84 and line 110 here: https://github.com/CCS-Lab/Haines_2020_CPS/blob/master/Code/Stan/trait_explanatory_model_testset.stan <https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://github.com/CCS-Lab/Haines_2020_CPS/blob/master/Code/Stan/trait_explanatory_model_testset.stan__;!!KGKeukY!n-p3AxwpmRteFZG9fHr366Cp4m6hNHgORey6W45a9C27mLvyYEwWSdFmVx8ub1FcLeLnttmciAFl$>. The extension is to set up a matrix/array that has rows for participants, and then columns for trials within participants. Then, instead of summing the likelihood over trials within participants, you can save the log likelihood for each trial separately.
>
> If you take the above approach, one the model is done fitting, you can extract the log likelihood array and then actually compute LOOIC for each participant and model independently, or you can calculate LOOIC on the whole array simultaneously, which is akin to something like leave-one-trial of one-participant out.
>
> If you do not go this route (or even if you do), I think doing the posterior predictions or other forms of simulation to critique the model would be best. Typically, statistical model fit is not the only thing worth considering, and using a holistic set of approaches may be best to answer your scientific question.
>
